{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "from typing import List, Any\n\nclass SortThirdFunction:\n    \"\"\"\n    Encapsulates the algorithm that sorts elements at indices divisible by three.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def sort_third(l: List[Any]) -> List[Any]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Return a new list where elements at indices 0, 3, 6, \u2026 are sorted ascending.\n        All other elements remain in their original positions.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not l:\n            return []\n\n        indices = [i for i in range(len(l)) if i % 3 == 0]\n        sorted_values = sorted(l[i] for i in indices)\n\n        result = l.copy()\n        for i, val in zip(indices, sorted_values):\n            result[i] = val\n\n        return result\n\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    return SortThirdFunction.sort_third(l)"}
